.TH INET 5N UniSoft
.SH NAME
inet \- Internet protocol family
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Internet protocol family is a collection of protocols
layered atop the
.I "Internet Protocol\^" 
.RI ( \s-1IP\s+1\^ )
transport layer, and utilizing the Internet address format.
The Internet family provides protocol support for the
.SM SOCK_STREAM\*S, 
.SM SOCK_DGRAM\*S, 
and 
.SM SOCK_RAW 
socket types; the
.SM SOCK_RAW
interface provides access to the 
.SM 
.I IP\^
protocol.
.SH ADDRESSING
Internet addresses are four byte quantities, stored in
network standard format.
The include file 
.RI < \|net/in.h\| > 
defines this address
as a discriminated union with the following conventions,
.PP
.nf
.ta \w'/'u  +\w'*/\ 'u
/*
	*	Internet address
	*/
.PP
struct in_addr {
	union {
		struct { u_char s_b1,s_b2,s_b3,s_b4; } S_un_b;
		struct { u_short s_w1,s_w2; } S_un_w;
		u_long S_addr;
	} S_un;
};
.fi
.DT
.PP
Sockets bound to the Internet protocol family utilize
the following addressing structure,
.PP
.nf
.ta \w'struct\ 'u +\w'sockaddr_in {\ \ \ \ 'u
struct	sockaddr_in {
	short	sin_family;
	u_short	sin_port;
	struct	in_addr sin_addr;
	char	sin_zero[8];
};
.fi
.PP
Sockets may be created with the address
.SM INADDR_ANY
to effect "wildcard" matching on incoming messages. 
.SH PROTOCOLS
The Internet protocol family is comprised of
the 
.SM
.I IP\^
transport protocol, Internet Control
Message Protocol 
.RI ( \s-1ICMP\s+1\^ ),
Transmission Control Protocol 
.RI ( \s-1TCP\s+1\^ ),
and User Datagram Protocol 
.RI ( \s-1UDP\s+1\^ ).
.SM
.I TCP\^ 
is used to support the 
.SM SOCK_STREAM
abstraction while 
.SM
.I UDP\^ 
is used to support the 
.SM SOCK_DGRAM
abstraction.  A raw interface to 
.SM 
.I IP\^
is available
by creating an Internet socket of type 
.SM SOCK_RAW\*S.
The 
.SM 
.I ICMP\^
message protocol is not directly accessible.
.SH INTERFACES
A number of interfaces are usable with the Internet
protocol family.  These include 
various Ethernet interfaces
and standard a
"software loopback" interface.
.SH SEE ALSO
ip(5N),
lo(5N)
tcp(5N), 
udp(5N).
.\"N	 @(#)inet.5N	 5.2 of 7/12/84
